## Alkyl Chain Regulated Charge Transfer in Fluorescent Inorganic CsPbBr\(_3\) Perovskite Solar Cells
The laboratory-scale organometallic halide perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have achieved a power conversion efficiency (PCE) as high as \(25.2\%\), which is comparable to those of state-of-the-art silicon or thin-film photovoltaics.[1-4] However, the notorious long-term instability is still a great burden for their commercialization under realistic operation conditions.[1,6] Enormous strategies including composition engineering, encapsulation technology and surface stabilization etc have been performed to enhance the device stability,[7-9] however the thermodynamically spontaneous ion migration of volatile organic components still causes an inevitable performance degradation under the stimuli of light irradiation and/or electric field.[10] Therefore, the full substitution of organic species by inorganic cesium ions to form all-inorganic cesium lead halide perovskites is considered as a promising solution to stabilize the device performance.[11-13] Although the optical bandgap of all-inorganic perovskites can be cut down to maximize light absorption by increasing iodine dosage, the lattice stability is adverse owing to the inferior tolerance factor. Among them, all-brominated CsPbBr\(_3\) perovskite demonstrates a superior tolerance to moisture, light, heat and/or oxygen, but the large bandgap of \(2.3\,\mathsf{eV}\) and energy barrier of \(0.6\,\mathsf{eV}\) at CsPbBr\(_3\)/carbon interface in state-of-the-art carbon-based CsPbBr\(_3\) PSCs undoubtedly cause a narrow spectral absorption and sluggish charge carrier extraction.[14-16]
p-type quantum dots (QDs) decorated with organic ligands are always used to set an intermediate energy edge at CsPbBr\(_3\)/carbon interface because of their band distribution, adjustability and superior charge extraction ability. The surface charge properties of QDs determine their optoelectronic behaviors, especially for the alkyl acid decorated QDs.[17] These organic ligands help QDs better use in photoelectric devices such as providing essential colloidal stability and solution processibility, coordinating to surface atoms and passivating surface defects as well as the absolute energy level shift of QDs.[18] The alkyl chain length of ligands also determines the charge-transfer properties between QDs and there is a trade-off between charge recombination and transfer properties. Therefore, precise control of surface coordination chemistry allows to better understand interfacial charge extraction-transfer mechanism which enables rational design of surface ligands decorated QDs in photovoltaics. In this work, core/shell structured CuInS\(_2\)/ZnS QD with valence band of \(\sim5.25\,\mathrm{eV}\) is used as a hole-conductor in carbon-based CsPbBr\(_3\) PSC. The charge transfer kinetics is maximized and the charge recombination is minimized by regulating alkyl chain length of organic ligands, which in turn enhances the cell efficiency owing to the balance of surface dipole induced charge coulomb repulsive force and quantum tunneling distance.
In addition, some strategies such as the incorporation of functional materials with wide-spectral absorbance range,[19] or fabricating spectrum-complementary sub-cells[20] have been developed to improve spectral response of solar cells. A reasonable mechanism is mainly attributed to the re-absorption or conversion of low-energy photos by functional photoactive layers.[21-24] Inspired by this concept, we further present a fluorescent carbon electrode by means of incorporating long persistence phosphor (LPP) into a carbon ink for a cathode to improve the PCE output. Arising from their light-storage and light-emitting behaviors, the unabsorbed incident light is expected to be converted into fluorescent with a wavelength of \(516\,\mathsf{nm}\), which in turn increases the light harvesting ability of CsPbBr\(_3\) solar cell. Finally, a champion PCE as high as \(10.85\%\) is achieved in the optimized solar cell, which is much higher than \(7.38\%\) for reference device.
The efficiency optimization is attributed to the regulation of ligand-induced surface charge transfer dynamics owing to the interaction between ligands and perovskite.[26] Obviously, the calculated chain lengths of ligands increase linearly from 6.84 Å to \(31.9\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) with respect to the number of carbon atom, as shown in Figure 1e. Longer alkyl chain is detrimental to effective charge
transfer across perovskite/QDs interfaces.[27] On the basis of photovoltaic characterization, the charge-transfer process is accelerated by decorating C12-QDs at \(\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}/\) carbon interface, and then decreased for 18 and 24 - \(\mathsf{-C H}_{2}\) groups capped QDs. This corresponds to the quantum tunneling layer between perovskite and QDs, which can significantly increase device performance by suppressing carrier recombination.[28] Once the quantum tunneling layer gets too thick \((>\uparrow6.9\,\mathrm{~\AA~}\) for \(12\ -\mathsf{C H}_{2}\) groups), the hindered electron transfer at interface causes a low fill factor and an inferior efficiency, which is highly accordant with reported thickness of \(\sim\!2\,\textrm{n m}\) in literature.[29] By contrary, the large charge coulomb repulsive force between surface dipole induced positive charge centers and photogenerated holes induces sluggish charge extraction in the case of ligands with short alkyl chain. By calculating the coulomb repulsive force \((F)\) according to \(F\ =\ q_{1}q_{2}/4\pi\varepsilon_{0}r^{2}\) , where \(q_{1}\) and \(q_{2}\) are charge quantity, \(\varepsilon_{0}\) is permittivity of vacuum, and \(r\) is distance between two charge centers, the \(F\) reduces from \(1.97\!\times\!10^{-9}\) N for \(4\ -\mathsf{C H}_{2}\) capped QDs to \(6.53\!\times\!10^{-10}\) N, \(3.23\!\times\!10^{-10}\) N, \(1.54\!\times\!10^{-10}\) N and \(9.11\!\times\!10^{-11}\) N for 8, 12, 18, and 24 - \(\mathsf{-C H}_{2}\) capped QDs, respectively. Finally, a compensatory balance between quantum tunneling distance (charge transfer) and surface dipole induced charge coulomb repulsive force (charge extraction) is achieved in C12-QDs tailored device for a maximized efficiency.
